{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target requires a 'divergent internal audit trail' as the sole evidentiary marker separating genuine reliability gain from mere compliance-tracking, but this criterion is neither necessary nor sufficient for the problem's stated question. (1) Not sufficient: a non-expert panel can produce documented near-misses or resolved contradictions while still merely mechanically executing checklist items in the final step—internal-divergence evidence alone does not establish that the panel exercised improved *judgment* rather than compliance that happened to surface intermediate conflicts. (2) Not necessary: genuine reliability improvement can be demonstrated within a single evaluation via an orthogonal criterion the target never considers—comparing the panel's accuracy on checklist items that are causally relevant to the outcome against items that are spurious or decoy; if the panel's marks track relevant cues and ignore irrelevant ones, that discriminates genuine signal-use from compliance-tracking without requiring any internal-audit-trail divergence. Under a task with objectively verifiable outcomes and zero near-misses (clean linear path), the target's framework would classify the result as 'illusory' by default, even if the panel's selective attention to relevant checklist items vs decoys shows the gain was genuine.","counterexample":[{"evidence":"panel correctly marks causally relevant checklist items (income, debt-ratio) and leaves spurious/decoy items (applicant's stated hobbies) blank, at a higher rate than the expert, yielding higher accuracy","expert":"single domain expert relying on intuition","panel":"trained non-experts with checklist, no internal contradictions, clean linear path","problem_asked":"distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance that happens to track the right answer","target_prediction":"'illusory compliance tracking' because no divergent audit trail","task":"verify whether a loan application meet